{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/839f92618d199653abe4d5ec90b8c2bdc89e67b9b8ca491f21605ad07ad5a18d.jpg\nCaption: Table 4: Ablation study results on DocBench. The “Chunk-only” variant bypasses dual-graph construction and relies solely on traditional chunk-based retrieval, while “w/o Reranker” eliminates cross-modal reranking but preserves the core graph-based architecture.\nStructure: <table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Method</td><td colspan=\"5\">Domains</td><td colspan=\"3\">Types</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Overall</td></tr><tr><td>Aca.</td><td>Fin.</td><td>Gov.</td><td>Law.</td><td>News</td><td>Txt.</td><td>Mm.</td><td>Una.</td></tr><tr><td>Chunk-only</td><td>55.8</td><td>61.5</td><td>60.1</td><td>60.7</td><td>64.0</td><td>81.6</td><td>66.2</td><td>43.5</td><td>60.0</td></tr><tr><td>w/o Reranker</td><td>60.9</td><td>63.5</td><td>58.8</td><td>60.2</td><td>68.6</td><td>81.7</td><td>74.7</td><td>45.4</td><td>62.4</td></tr><tr><td>RAGAnything</td><td>61.4</td><td>67.0</td><td>61.5</td><td>60.2</td><td>66.3</td><td>85.0</td><td>76.3</td><td>46.0</td><td>63.4</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: The table in question is Table 4 from the document, which presents ablation study results on the DocBench dataset. The table is structured with a header row and a header column, providing a clear and organized layout. The header row includes 'Method' and 'Domains' (Academic, Financial, Government, Law, News) and 'Types' (Text, Multimodal, Unimodal), while the 'Overall' column provides a summary of the performance across all domains and types. The rows below the header represent different methods: 'Chunk-only', 'w/o Reranker', and 'RAGAnything'. \n\nKey data points and patterns include:\n- The 'Chunk-only' method, which bypasses dual-graph construction, achieves lower accuracy across all domain